methods_html <- function(nsim = 2000) {
  nsim_txt <- if (nsim == 2000) "Two thousand" else format(nsim, big.mark = ",")
  paste0(
    "<p><b>Sources.</b> Google Scholar profile pages (through SerpAPI or a local scrape) give per paper citation counts, ",
    "the profile's citations per year, and Scholar's own h and i10. OpenAlex gives DOIs, author lists and order, ",
    "citations per year from 2012 onward, field weighted citation impact (FWCI), and citation percentiles. ",
    "Scholar and OpenAlex counts are never mixed inside one metric; each chart names its source. OpenAlex datasets (Zenodo, Figshare ",
    "and Dataverse deposits) are left out, and OpenAlex only records sharing a title and year are folded into one work.</p>",
    "<p><b>Matching.</b> Scholar entries pair with OpenAlex works by DOI, then exact normalized title, then fuzzy title ",
    "(token sort ratio of at least 92 that beats the runner up by 3), one to one, within a year of each other. Anything left ",
    "over is listed under Data, never guessed. Manual overrides (merge, exclude, set DOI, set OpenAlex id) are recorded once ",
    "and reapplied on every run.</p>",
    "<p><b>Indices.</b> h, g (padded with uncited papers as in Publish or Perish, so g can exceed the paper count), i10, e, ",
    "contemporary h (Sidiropoulos et al. 2007, gamma 4, delta 1), hI norm, hm, AWCRpA and the per author counts (over papers ",
    "with a known author count only, which Scholar entries have once matched to OpenAlex; the share covered is stored), ",
    "annual h, AWCR and AW, m quotient (h over academic age), and citation velocity (least squares slope ",
    "of weekly cumulative citations over the past year, shown once eight weekly points exist). Year end values before tracking ",
    "began are reconstructed from OpenAlex per work yearly counts and drawn dashed.</p>",
    "<p><b>Annualized indices.</b> Rates per career year (papers, g, i10, citations, alongside the m quotient) and per paper ",
    "year (mean and median of citations divided by years since publication) compare authors at different career stages. ",
    "The five year window is the last five complete calendar years before the snapshot, always from OpenAlex per work yearly ",
    "counts so every author is measured on one source: citations received in it, citations per year, h5 and i10 over it, and ",
    "papers published in it with the citations they hold, their h, and citations per recent paper. Scholar's own h5 and i10 ",
    "cover six calendar years including the partial current one and are kept apart as reported values.</p>",
    "<p><b>Forecasts.</b> Each paper's annual citation rate comes from a Poisson log linear fit to its last six complete years ",
    "of OpenAlex counts (at least four years and five citations). Slopes are shrunk toward the author's slope distribution by ",
    "empirical Bayes, growth beyond the last complete year is damped by a factor of 0.8 a year, and the slope is bounded to between ",
    "minus 39% and plus 49% a year; uncertainty combines parameter draws with negative binomial count noise. Younger or sparser ",
    "papers use a Gamma posterior on their latest year's rate, with the exposure of a paper dated the current year floored at a quarter ",
    "year; unmatched papers use their Scholar lifetime rate or weekly velocity. ",
    "Rates convert to Scholar units with each paper's Scholar to OpenAlex ratio shrunk toward the author's. ", nsim_txt, " simulations ",
    "give 80% and 95% intervals for h, i10 and citations one to three years ahead. Only papers already on the record are projected, ",
    "so totals are conservative for active authors. Every refresh backtests the model: refit with counts through the year ",
    "before last, predict last year, and compare with what OpenAlex recorded (in OpenAlex units, so the Scholar scaling is not ",
    "tested). Every projected value in the tables, heatmap, statements and value boxes is shown only when that backtest lands ",
    "within a factor of 1.5; the fan charts and the portfolio point sizes stay visible with the check's verdict beside them.</p>",
    "<p><b>Comparisons.</b> Academic age counts from the first publication year (or the year set in authors.yml). Each author is ",
    "shown in Google Scholar units when a Scholar profile is tracked and OpenAlex units otherwise; heatmap columns carry the unit, ",
    "rows marked (OA) are OpenAlex for everyone, and the caption warns when the selection mixes units. Heatmap shading ranks ",
    "each metric within the selected set only. Concentration curves and the Gini coefficient describe how citations spread across papers.</p>"
  )
}
